  "readmeFile": "nuget_readme.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Single package for legacy and modern targets. The netstandard2.0 build (2.x line) and the\nnet8.0+ build (10.x line) are merged into one package targeting netstandard2.0, net8.0, net9.0 and net10.0,\nso .NET Framework 4.6.2+ / .NET Standard 2.0 consumers now get the same API surface as .NET 8+ consumers.\n\nThe async methods previously exclusive to the 10.x line (ExistAsync, ExistsAsync, DownloadAsync,\nDownloadAsStringAsync, DownloadAsStreamAsync, UploadAsync, DeleteAsync, ListFilesAsync, GetFileInfoAsync,\nDisposeAsync) are now available on netstandard2.0 as well.\n\nNote for 2.x users upgrading: Dispose() now calls GC.SuppressFinalize, and SFtpClient additionally\nimplements IAsyncDisposable. No existing member was removed or changed.\n\nDependencies are now open-ended ranges with raised floors, so that a consumer who references only\nthis package is not left resolving a flagged version they would otherwise have to pin themselves:\n\n* SSH.NET floor is 2026.0.0, the first release carrying the fix for GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284 (arbitrary\n  file write via server-controlled filenames during ScpClient recursive download). This library\n  only uses SftpClient and never ScpClient, so the advisory was not reachable through its API, but\n  the transitive warning still reached consumers.\n* Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions floor is 10.0.11, raising the 8.0.3 that SSH.NET asks\n  for off the .NET 8 servicing line before it leaves support in November 2026. This also carries\n  Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions from 8.0.2 to 10.0.11.\n* Microsoft.Bcl.AsyncInterfaces floor is 10.0.11 (netstandard2.0 only).\n* System.IO.Hashing floor is 10.0.11, and on the legacy path System.Formats.Asn1 and\n  Microsoft.Bcl.Cryptography are 10.0.11 as well.\n\nEvery package in the resulting graph resolves to its latest stable release, on all four targets,\nand none has a known advisory against it.\n\nOne addition to note for .NET Framework and .NET Standard 2.0 consumers: the newer\nLogging.Abstractions brings System.Diagnostics.DiagnosticSource into the graph, so that assembly\nnow ships alongside the rest. It has no known advisories.",
